      "why_read": "Learn how offloading filesystem semantics directly into SSD controllers eliminates kernel overhead and host CPU bottlenecks for accelerator-driven workloads.",
      "hn_id": "50182877",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50182877",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Modern accelerators can move gigabytes of data over PCIe without the host CPU, yet the control path remains choked by the kernel filesystem. In high-throughput GPU workloads, translating filenames into block addresses and checking permissions can burn up to 30 percent of host CPU cycles.\n\nFADU's FS-SSD takes an aggressive approach to this bottleneck by embedding an entire NFSv3 file engine directly on the storage controller. Instead of serving raw blocks, the drive interprets NFS operations encapsulated directly within standard NVMe commands, entirely bypassing the host block layer.\n\nThis architecture allows GPUs, SmartNICs, and DPUs to stream files by name directly from NVMe-over-Fabrics targets without involving a storage-server CPU. Storage engineers operating large-scale distributed clusters should track this architectural shift closely, as it represents a fundamental rethink of where the filesystem boundary belongs."
