    {
      "title": "PHP memory limit bailouts cause remote code execution",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://markdown.page/AzvY2d69",
      "date": "2026-10-21",
      "authors": [
        "yuvadam"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this analysis to understand how PHP aborting requests via longjmp leaves corrupted state across persistent worker processes, enabling unauthenticated remote code execution. You will gain a deep mechanistic view of Zend engine internals and cross-request state vulnerabilities.",
      "hn_id": "50181215",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50181215",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Process reuse models rely on clean state teardown between requests, but unmanaged memory aborts can break this boundary completely. A major vulnerability disclosure in the PHP Zend engine shows how memory exhaustion leads directly to unauthenticated remote code execution.\n\nHistorically, PHP enforced memory limits by calling zend_bailout, which executes a longjmp without unwinding C call stacks. When a worker process exhausts its memory allocation mid-operation, any data structure currently under construction is left in a corrupted or half-initialized state.\n\nBecause persistent worker pools like PHP-FPM and prefork Apache reuse the same process to serve subsequent requests, these dangling pointers and corrupt structures survive into the next request lifecycle. An attacker can deliberately trigger an allocation failure and then groom the heap on the next request to trigger use-after-free execution.\n\nRelying on non-local jumps for error recovery in persistent environments creates catastrophic side effects. Robust isolation requires explicit resource unwinding or dedicated process boundaries."
    },
    {
