      "image": "/infographics/06-hn-50122039.jpg",
      "content": "Container and virtual machine cold starts are rarely limited by compute initialization; they are almost always bottlenecked by filesystem preparation. Modal achieved sub-second cold starts for their VM sandboxes by rethinking how disk images are distributed and mounted.\n\nInstead of pulling and unpacking complete multi-gigabyte layers before starting execution, their ImageFS architecture loads only the bare metadata index upfront. A custom FUSE server written in Rust handles initial file queries in milliseconds while lazily streaming file contents on demand.\n\nThe storage engine employs a tiered content-addressed cache spanning local memory, regional caches, and object storage. Background prefetching anticipates disk access patterns so that subsequent tool calls in agentic workloads execute against local memory rather than remote storage.\n\nMoving file transfer out of the critical path turns cold start latency from minutes into milliseconds."
