    {
      "title": "How cascading deletes trigger massive production database outages",
      "source": "hn",
      "url": "https://planetscale.com/blog/i-dont-like-delete-cascade",
      "date": "2026-10-21",
      "authors": [
        "Tom Pang"
      ],
      "why_read": "Read this to understand the hidden transactional and locking hazards of using cascading deletes at scale. You will learn how unchecked foreign key cascades can collide with routine schema migrations to trigger catastrophic database outages.",
      "hn_id": "50183505",
      "comments": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50183505",
      "image": null,
      "content": "Postgres cascading deletes seem harmless until an unindexed foreign key meets a concurrent schema migration in production. A single top-level delete can silently lock entire table hierarchies, turning a routine cleanup into an availability incident.\n\nWhen the database engine processes an ON DELETE CASCADE clause, it does not simply erase rows from storage. It executes nested deletes row by row, generating massive write-ahead log volume, updating secondary indexes, and producing dead tuples that trigger aggressive autovacuum pressure. Crucially, every modified row holds exclusive row-level locks throughout the parent transaction.\n\nIf a deployment script runs an ALTER TABLE statement during this cascading wipe, the data definition lock queues behind those row locks. Any subsequent read or write query targeting those tables blocks behind the migration lock. Within seconds, connection pools saturate and services time out.\n\nHandling bulk deletions in application batches or background workers requires more boilerplate code, but it decouples row removal from schema operations.\n\nExplicit cleanup prevents cascading locks from taking down your database cluster."
    },
