      "image": null,
      "content": "Public LLM leaderboards tell you almost nothing about how an agent will actually perform inside your private codebase. Most teams ship a harness, customized repository context, specific system instructions, and task routing rather than an isolated foundation model.\n\nAn engineering experiment mining twenty-four real tasks directly from git history revealed a 0.96 correlation with Cognition FrontierCode rankings across ten shared configurations. While the relative model rankings transferred remarkably well, the operating costs did not. Tasks run on private code cost between three and five times more per run than public benchmark numbers indicated.\n\nThis divergence reveals critical architectural takeaways for production agent systems. High-end reasoning models like Claude Opus and GPT-6.1 Sol achieved near-identical success rates on internal tasks, yet the cost differed by a factor of six. Furthermore, routing smaller, scoped bug fixes to lightweight models preserved solution quality while cutting token spend drastically.\n\nBuilding an internal benchmark from your repository history provides the ground truth required to optimize model routing and harness configuration. Relying purely on public scores or developer intuition will cost you thousands of dollars in unnecessary token burn."
