      "content": "Fixing edge cases in production machine learning models is notoriously tedious. Engineers patch one obscure failure mode only to watch a nearly identical sample break the pipeline the following week.\n\nThe team behind OXR approached this problem systematically by implementing a recursive self-improvement loop for document parsing. Instead of relying on manual curation, they feed unlabeled documents through the parser multiple times while introducing subtle perturbations like scaling, blur, and compression. If the predictions diverge, the page is flagged as unstable.\n\nFrom there, a separate vision-language model inspects the mismatches and generates failure hypotheses. These hypotheses are clustered across the dataset to uncover systemic blind spots, which then steer synthetic data generation for subsequent post-training iterations.\n\nAutomating failure detection through disagreement and perturbation provides a solid blueprint for continuous model hardening in production."
