      "content": "Ingesting a two-billion-row table from MySQL into an event stream or materialized view engine often hits a wall during initial snapshotting. When table dumps take forty hours, binlogs expire before change data capture can even start, causing replication to fail completely.\n\nThe standard solution is splitting primary keys across parallel workers. However, simple chunking falls apart on text or composite keys. Relying on MySQL explain estimates or index dives produces wildly skewed partitions, where one worker handles millions of rows while another finishes instantly. The Materialize team solved this by using an adaptive prefix probing algorithm. Instead of guessing boundary values or scanning huge offsets, the partitioner probes B-tree index depths using string prefixes to discover balanced row splits.\n\nThis technique delivered a tenfold speedup on massive tables while avoiding collation edge cases that quietly corrupt range boundaries.\n\nWhen standard pagination primitives fail under terabyte workloads, probing index structures directly is how you win."
